18.🧩 What is Bootstrap?
Bootstrap is a sampling technique used to create multiple subsets of a dataset by randomly selecting data points with replacement.

📘 Meaning of “with replacement”:

If you pick a data point from the dataset, you put it back before picking the next one.
So, the same data point can appear multiple times in one sample

19.🌳 What is Random Forest?

Random Forest is an ensemble method that uses both Bagging and Bootstrap concepts with Decision Trees.
It builds many decision trees and combines their results to make a final prediction.

⚙️ How Random Forest Works:

a)Bootstrap Sampling:
Create many random subsets of the data using bootstrap.

b)Train Decision Trees:
Train one decision tree per subset.

c)Feature Randomness:
At each tree split, choose a random subset of features instead of using all features — this makes trees more diverse.

Aggregation:

For classification → takes majority vote of trees.
For regression → takes average of tree predictions.

20.🌟 1️⃣ Gradient Boosting
💡 Definition:
Gradient Boosting builds models step by step, and each new model tries to minimize the errors (residuals) of the previous model using gradient descent.
That’s why it’s called “Gradient” Boosting.

🚀 2️⃣ XGBoost (Extreme Gradient Boosting)
💡 Definition:
XGBoost is an optimized and improved version of Gradient Boosting designed for speed and performance.
It was created to overcome the limitations (slow speed & overfitting) of traditional Gradient Boosting.

21. Hierarchical Clustering (Tree-based Clustering)
👉 Simple Meaning
It builds a tree-like structure (dendrogram) by repeatedly joining or splitting clusters.

Two Types

Agglomerative (Bottom-up) → most used
Start: each point is its own cluster
Step-by-step: merge the two closest clusters
End: everything becomes one big cluster
Divisive (Top-down)
Start: one big cluster
Step-by-step: split into smaller clusters

👉 Output
A dendrogram (tree) where you can “cut” at any height to choose how many clusters you want.

👉 Best for when:
You want to see how clusters form step-by-step
You don’t know the number of clusters beforehand
You want a visual structure of clusters

22. DBSCAN (Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise)
👉 Simple Meaning

It groups points that are close and dense.
Points in low-density areas become noise/outliers.

👉 Works based on two things:

eps → maximum distance to consider neighbors
min_samples → minimum points to form a dense region (cluster)

👉 How it works

Pick a point
Count neighbors within eps
If neighbors ≥ min_samples → it becomes a core point
Expand cluster by adding nearby points
Points not reachable become noise

👉 Best for when:

Data has irregular shapes (circles, curves, clusters like moon-shape)
You want automatic outlier detection
You do NOT want to manually select k (unlike KMeans)
